  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will prevail through Tuesday.\n\nGood radiational cooling taking place across the area with \ntemperatures in the 50s and even 40s in the cooler spots. Some \nfog has also developed in the valleys which will dissipate after\nsunrise and as mixing takes place. \n\nOtherwise...a absolutely beautiful day will unfold with high \npressure over the area promoting sun-filled skies and temperatures \nexpected in the 70s this afternoon. \n\nTonight...again some fog will once again be found in the valleys \nacross the area. Lows will be in the 50s to mid-upper 40s in the \ncooler locales. \n\nTuesday...high pressure will continue to maintain mainly dry quite \nweather across much of the eastern Great Lakes. Although...we may \nsee some clouds push in across the St. Lawrence Valley and eastern \nLake Ontario region as a back door front drops across that region. \nHi-res guidance suggest we even could see an isolated shower there \nbut confidence of this occuring is low. Highs on Tuesday will be a \ntad bit warmer with mid to upper 70s across the region. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the eastern Great Lakes \nWednesday through Wednesday night, with a chance of showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nA sharp mid level trough will advance east across Ontario and Quebec \nWednesday through Wednesday night, with the southern extent of \nheight falls spreading east across the Great Lakes region. A narrow \naxis of greater moisture (PW values 2.0 to 2.1 inches) will be \nadvect into the eastern Great Lakes just ahead of the system, with \nsurface dewpoints rising into the mid to upper 60s by Wednesday \nafternoon and contributing to at least some instability. A pre-\nfrontal trough is forecast to advance east across the eastern Great \nLakes Wednesday afternoon or Wednesday night, with some timing \ndifferences still showing in latest model guidance. \n\nDeep moisture and forcing along and ahead of the mid level trough \nand pre-frontal trough will bring increasing chances of showers and \nthunderstorms later Wednesday through Wednesday night. Shear will \nalso increase as a belt of enhanced mid level flow near the base of \nthe trough moves into the area. There may be at least some limited \nsevere potential if the timing of forcing aligns with the diurnal \nheating cycle and sufficient instability develops.\n\nWith the passage of the front Wednesday night we should see drier \nweather slowly return Thursday but a few showers may linger. Cooler \nand dry Friday as high pressure settles in across the eastern Great \nLakes.",
